==English==
{{wikidata lexeme|L757}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|rehersen}}, from {{der|en|xno|reherser||to repeat word-for-word}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈhɜːs/|a=RP}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-rehearse.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈhɝs/|a=GA}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[repeat]], as what has been already said; to tell over again; to [[recite]].
#: {{ux|en|There’s no need to '''rehearse''' the same old argument; we’ve heard it before, and we all agree.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[narrate]]; to [[relate]]; to [[tell]]; to recount.
#: {{ux|en|The witness '''rehearsed''' the events of the night before for the listening detectives.}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[practise]] by recitation or repetition in private for experiment and improvement, prior to a public representation, especially in [[theater]].
#: {{ux|en|The main actors spent on average two hours a day '''rehearsing''' before the first night.}}
#: {{ux|en|The lawyer advised her client to '''rehearse''' her testimony before the trial date.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1648|author={{w|Robert Herrick (poet)|Robert Herrick}}|title=Hesperides|chapter=When he would have his verses read|passage=In sober mornings, do not thou '''reherse''' / The holy incantation of a verse ...}}
#* {{RQ:Fielding Pasquin|page=1|passage=I ſuppoſe vve ſhall hardly '''Rehearſe''' the Comedy this Morning; for the Author vvas Arreſted as he vvas going home from ''King's Coffee-houſe''; and, as I heard, it vvas for upvvards of Four Pound: I ſuppoſe he vvill hardly get Bail.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|theatre}} To cause to rehearse; to instruct by [[rehearsal]].
#: {{ux|en|The director '''rehearsed''' the cast incessantly in the days leading up to opening night, and as a result they were tired and cranky when it arrived.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Tale of Two Cities|chapter=Darkness|page=231|passage=He {{...}} has been '''rehearsed''' by Madame Defarge as to his having seen Her {{...}}}}
# To [[contrive]] and carefully prepare (a story, etc.) to offer consistency.
#: {{ux|en|The Crown argued that the accused had '''rehearsed''' her story.}}

